{
  "about": "An XML file of a file data source as one table, through Clarity.Formats (docs/FORMATS-PLAN.md, \"XML\", \"A file as a data source\"). `at` names the records with slashes, first the root, last the record element; without it the root's child elements are the records. The first column, `#node`, is each record's element as its XML text, so writing back keeps the attributes and elements no field maps; then one column per field the entity maps, in the order given. A column that is a plain name is found as a child element of that name, else an attribute of that name (names by their local name, exact first, then ignoring case, underscores, hyphens and spaces); `@a` is the attribute only, `a/b` the text of an element inside, `a/@b` its attribute, `.` the record's own text. Nothing found, or empty, is null: a column found in no record is no error in XML. A field that is a record or a list of records is no column; it stays inside `#node`. A list of values is a column: the elements of its name, repeated, or the items inside one element of its name whose children all have one name; one empty element is an empty list; its cell is the list's JSON text. Cells are fitted as formats-table.json says. `write_table` puts row i in the place of old record i in the parent; a place left over is removed with the blank text before it, more rows follow the last record, each after a copy of that blank; with no records before, the rows go at the end of the parent. A row's element is its `#node`, or a new element named by the last step of `at`; each column's value is set where its column says (a plain name: the child element there, else the attribute there, else a new child element), made where missing; null empties an element's text or removes an attribute; a list's items are written as elements of its name in the old ones' places (inside the old wrapper when there was one), null removing them and an empty list one empty element; a yes or no is written true or false. Everything outside the records is kept; the declaration is written again with the original's encoding name (UTF-8 without one), the nodes around the root on their own lines when the original's first node ended its line. An empty original is a new file: the elements `at` names, laid out by `indent`, in the `encoding` given (UTF-8 without it).",
